DASHBOARD
-----
Dashboard:
Open Dashboard_V2.py in Python 3.11
Install required packages: pip install pandas folium fiona shapely pyproj rtree dash
Run Dashboard_V2.py: python Dashboard_V2.py
-----

MODEL
-----
To use function written by for prediction you have to:

1 . 
train_burglary_classifier_with_6plus_regression(db_path='crime_data.db', model_path='models/')
Where models is folder created in the code repository and crime_data.db is a dataset that I sent to you earlier
 
You should get folder models with those files inside
 
Also you get model performnce metrics.
Most important one for classification is accuracy and for regression - RMSE
2. 
predict_burglary_for_month(target_month, db_path='crime_data.db', model_path='models/')
Where you specify target_month in the following format: "2025-03"
Also you have to assign result to some variable, so you will be able to use dataset further.
Model can also predict data recursively, however reliability will decrease, as far as assumptions will be based on assumptions, however there is basically no limitation for prediction dates.
For further months(not next from the current date) you should addd notification that they are not highly reliable.

In the resulting table LSOA_code is already synchronized with WD24CD through geospatial data and predicted burglaries per LSOA for a certain month.
3. 
get_crime_coordinates_from_db(db_path='crime_data.db')

You assign result of this to a certain variable, will return you dataset with latitudes longitudes for future police officer LSOA centroids predictions
4. 
allocate_police_hours_stepwise_reduction_with_coords(df, crime_df, total_hours_per_ward=800, patrol_block=2, step_percent=0.10, reduction_per_step=0.03, max_steps=5)
df – dataframe from step 2
crime_df  - initial dataset from which we extract latitude and longitude data

total_hours_per_ward – weekly police officer hours per ward
patrol_block – police should patrol together, to make it more efficient and safer
step_percent – police change that results in 3% decrease in crimes
reduction_per_step – 3 % reduction coefficient
max_steps - 5 number of iterations that ILP performs before providing final result

Where Latitude and Longitude per LSOA is centroid for police allocation for each LSOA(the closer to center of  it area is the higher police attention is required)
